WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.236 --> 00:00:01.690
So we still tell ourselves,

00:00:01.690 --> 00:00:03.749
we still believe that in
a marketplace of ideas

00:00:03.749 --> 00:00:06.872
that good true content
is gonna rise to the top.

00:00:06.872 --> 00:00:08.683
But the interesting thing
about social platforms

00:00:08.683 --> 00:00:10.345
is that the algorithms are designed

00:00:10.345 --> 00:00:11.918
to amplify what's popular.

00:00:11.918 --> 00:00:14.944
So if you can game the algorithm
and get something trending

00:00:14.944 --> 00:00:18.216
we have these curatorial
services like Search,

00:00:18.216 --> 00:00:20.468
Trending, and the Recommendation Engine

00:00:20.468 --> 00:00:22.849
which don't fulfill the same
function as human editors

00:00:22.849 --> 00:00:25.606
because for better or worse
human editors are biased,

00:00:25.606 --> 00:00:28.233
but algorithms they're
biased and game-able.

00:00:28.233 --> 00:00:30.603
And so we have this interesting
environment we've created

00:00:30.603 --> 00:00:34.024
where the assumptions that have
kinda under-lied our beliefs

00:00:34.024 --> 00:00:36.940
and how good content and
messaging rises to top,

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:39.076
the sort of notion of Brandeis and Mill,

00:00:39.076 --> 00:00:41.635
that bad speech will overcome good speech,

00:00:41.635 --> 00:00:45.714
is really largely defunct
in the era of automated bots

00:00:45.714 --> 00:00:49.008
and the ability for anyone
to go and spam a hashtag

00:00:49.008 --> 00:00:53.035
and spread a message
regardless of voracity.

00:00:53.035 --> 00:00:54.425
We're at the point where
if you make it trend

00:00:54.425 --> 00:00:55.317
you make it true,

00:00:55.317 --> 00:00:57.937
because some journalist somewhere
is going to cover the fact

00:00:57.937 --> 00:01:00.269
that it was trending.

00:01:00.269 --> 00:01:02.910
And there's an insidiousness to this,

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:04.755
because as we've entered into this world

00:01:04.755 --> 00:01:07.287
of information glut,
people are really at a loss

00:01:07.287 --> 00:01:10.038
as to what to trust what sources are good.

00:01:10.038 --> 00:01:12.298
And so people become in
many ways, very reliant

00:01:12.298 --> 00:01:14.863
on the sort of circle of
people who are nearest to them

00:01:14.863 --> 00:01:16.648
and what those people happen to share.

00:01:16.648 --> 00:01:18.706
And this is why one of the challenges

00:01:18.706 --> 00:01:20.712
is where human biases intersect

00:01:20.712 --> 00:01:22.630
with technological mechanisms.

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:24.242
There's comfort in seeing things

00:01:24.242 --> 00:01:26.061
that reflect your world view.

00:01:26.061 --> 00:01:29.056
And so, when someone
shares content that is

00:01:29.056 --> 00:01:31.529
completely false or biased or misleading,

00:01:31.529 --> 00:01:34.066
the Facebook algorithm
recognizes that this is a share

00:01:34.066 --> 00:01:35.030
from a trusted friend,

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:38.104
and so it does in fact spread
it to the rest of your feed.

00:01:38.104 --> 00:01:39.748
So if you go in and look at truther pages,

00:01:39.748 --> 00:01:43.094
you see these kind of
classic tactics that play,

00:01:43.094 --> 00:01:45.519
you make a new visitor
feel a sense of belonging,

00:01:45.519 --> 00:01:46.873
you reinforce their world view,

00:01:46.873 --> 00:01:48.554
you congratulate them on being awake,

00:01:48.554 --> 00:01:51.065
they're welcomed into a group.

00:01:51.065 --> 00:01:53.117
And we kind a joke around about the fact

00:01:53.117 --> 00:01:55.089
that social networks
have disrupted the market

00:01:55.089 --> 00:01:56.197
for cult leaders.

00:01:56.197 --> 00:01:57.768
(audience laughs)

00:01:57.768 --> 00:02:01.246
And that's because
propagandists need an audience.

00:02:01.246 --> 00:02:02.464
It doesn't matter how
they get the audience,

00:02:02.464 --> 00:02:05.159
the only thing that matters
is that the audience exists.

00:02:05.159 --> 00:02:07.303
And so on social
networks, you can buy one,

00:02:07.303 --> 00:02:10.197
you can build one, or you can co-oped one.

00:02:10.197 --> 00:02:11.499
So there's a lot of different ways

00:02:11.499 --> 00:02:12.640
that you can run this playbook.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:15.852
Maybe you start with ads,
maybe you buy some ads

00:02:15.852 --> 00:02:18.823
and the call to action on
the ad is to like a page.

00:02:18.823 --> 00:02:21.033
And then I have you on my
page, you've all liked my page

00:02:21.033 --> 00:02:22.684
that means that Facebook
is gonna push you at least

00:02:22.684 --> 00:02:24.536
some of the things that
I share on the page.

00:02:24.536 --> 00:02:26.349
And in fact, I can tell
you to tweak a setting

00:02:26.349 --> 00:02:28.874
that make sure that my page
is the first thing you see.

00:02:28.874 --> 00:02:30.430
And then maybe you're
really active on my page

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:32.618
and you like other people
who comment on my page.

00:02:32.618 --> 00:02:34.160
And then Facebook actually suggests

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:37.192
that maybe you and your friends
should create a shared group

00:02:37.192 --> 00:02:38.704
to talk about my page.

00:02:38.704 --> 00:02:40.796
And then at that point, I
have you in a group also.

00:02:40.796 --> 00:02:42.586
And once you're in a
group, that conversation

00:02:42.586 --> 00:02:44.541
is largely happening, closed and private

00:02:44.541 --> 00:02:47.076
and so is very difficult for
people who do try to monitor

00:02:47.076 --> 00:02:50.096
the rise of unfortunate hate
groups and things like that

00:02:50.096 --> 00:02:51.464
to really see what's happening

00:02:51.464 --> 00:02:52.363
in the closed groups on Facebook.

00:02:52.363 --> 00:02:54.527
It sort of has become this space

00:02:54.527 --> 00:02:57.431
where we call it the kinda
radicalization engine

00:02:57.431 --> 00:02:58.884
as the groups...

00:02:58.884 --> 00:03:00.497
Once you're in a certain
number of these groups

00:03:00.497 --> 00:03:01.889
Facebook tries to feed you more groups

00:03:01.889 --> 00:03:04.494
that are like them, ultimately again,

00:03:04.494 --> 00:03:05.852
the goal is to have an audience

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:08.813
and so you can move that audience
from platform to platform

00:03:08.813 --> 00:03:10.857
you can use blogs, tracking pixels,

00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:13.787
the entire system is set
up such that I can find you

00:03:13.787 --> 00:03:16.405
and reach you where you are.

00:03:16.405 --> 00:03:18.317
And that's why when people say propaganda

00:03:18.317 --> 00:03:19.993
has always existed,
they're absolutely right.

00:03:19.993 --> 00:03:22.669
But what has not always existed is

00:03:22.669 --> 00:03:26.094
inexpensive, sophisticated,
precision targeting.

00:03:26.094 --> 00:03:28.961
Environments where I know
who is likely to be receptive

00:03:28.961 --> 00:03:30.579
to my message, so I can build my audience

00:03:30.579 --> 00:03:33.296
remarkably inexpensively
and remarkably efficiently,

00:03:33.296 --> 00:03:36.520
precisely because the
Facebook ads interface

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.164
will serve my message to the
person who needs to see it

00:03:39.164 --> 00:03:41.790
for relatively small sums of money.

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:44.373
 

